    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d588">

                <pb facs="0666" n="656" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg656"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M)/14088a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Atcheson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-04T22:00:00-04:00">August 4, 1943—10
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1033. WPB<anchor n="19" xml:id="d588-f1"/> wishes following information. Should
                    we continue to give priority to Russian owned mercury and tin on American planes
                    and if so what is best estimate as to amounts available monthly. Mercury now has
                    A–1 priority and present schedule 80,000 pounds monthly: tin has A–2 with
                    monthly schedule 1,500,000 pounds.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d588-f1" n="19" xml:id="d588-f1-content">
                <p>War Production Board.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
